1
00:00:09,151 --> 00:00:12,352
The prototype has
passed every test, sir.

2
00:00:12,392 --> 00:00:14,250
It's working.

3
00:00:14,065 --> 00:00:16,503
I hardly think "working"

4
00:00:16,543 --> 00:00:18,107
is the correct word.

5
00:00:20,581 --> 00:00:24,774
That would apply only to machines.

6
00:00:24,814 --> 00:00:28,221
I'm sorry. I should say "it's alive".

7
00:00:30,289 --> 00:00:32,808
Can it hear me?
It might still be in shock.

8
00:00:32,848 --> 00:00:35,925
Bear in mind the brain has
been welded to the exoskeleton.

9
00:00:35,965 --> 00:00:38,962
Skin of metal. . .

10
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:47,432
. .and a body
that will never age or die.

11
00:00:47,472 --> 00:00:51,384
I envy it. Do you know me?

12
00:00:51,424 --> 00:00:53,345
Answer.

13
00:00:53,385 --> 00:00:55,661
Do you know me?

14
00:00:55,701 --> 00:00:58,983
You are John Lumic.

15
00:01:00,498 --> 00:01:02,160
My child.

16
00:01:02,056 --> 00:01:04,852
Mark this day, Dr Kendrick.

17
00:01:04,892 --> 00:01:06,809
We are blessed.

18
00:01:06,849 --> 00:01:10,447
Well, it's not quite over yet, sir.
We need Geneva's approval.

19
00:01:10,487 --> 00:01:15,284
Technically, this is a new
form of life, and that
contravenes the Bio-Convention.

20
00:01:15,324 --> 00:01:18,797
They need to ratify this.
And they will refuse me.

21
00:01:18,837 --> 00:01:20,595
Nonetheless, we need to tell them.

22
00:01:20,635 --> 00:01:24,871
And if I don't? Well, I'm sorry,
sir, but it's my duty.

23
00:01:24,911 --> 00:01:26,786
I shall have to inform them.

24
00:01:26,826 --> 00:01:31,462
And how will you do that
from beyond the grave?

25
00:01:31,502 --> 00:01:33,822
I don't quite understand.

26
00:01:33,862 --> 00:01:36,279
Kill him.

27
00:01:45,049 --> 00:01:47,608
Captain! Yes, sir?

28
00:01:47,648 --> 00:01:50,125
Inform all staff,

29
00:01:50,165 --> 00:01:53,202
we have a new destination.

30
00:01:53,242 --> 00:01:56,524
Set sail for Great Britain!

31
00:02:33,278 --> 00:02:36,794
The munchkin lady with the big eyes.
Remember how she looked at you?

32
00:02:36,834 --> 00:02:38,672
Then she opens her mouth
and fire comes out!

33
00:02:38,712 --> 00:02:40,232
I thought I was gonna get frazzled!

34
00:02:40,272 --> 00:02:43,787
Yeah, one minute she's
standing there, next minute, roar!

35
00:02:43,827 --> 00:02:45,623
Where was that, then? What happened?

36
00:02:45,663 --> 00:02:48,141
Oh, it was on this. . .

37
00:02:48,181 --> 00:02:51,617
this planet thing, asteroid. It's
a long story, you had to be there.

38
00:02:51,657 --> 00:02:54,550
Um. . . What are you doing that for?

39
00:02:54,095 --> 00:02:55,614
Cos you told me to.

40
00:02:55,654 --> 00:02:57,931
When was that?

41
00:02:57,971 --> 00:02:59,809
About half an hour ago.

42
00:02:59,849 --> 00:03:03,870
Um. . . You can let go now.

43
00:03:05,485 --> 00:03:08,420
Well, how long's it been
since I could've stopped?

44
00:03:08,082 --> 00:03:10,919
10 minutes. 20. . .

45
00:03:10,959 --> 00:03:12,677
29.

46
00:03:12,717 --> 00:03:15,274
You just forgot me!
No, no, no, I was just. . . I was. . .

47
00:03:15,314 --> 00:03:18,994
I was calibrating. I was just. . .
No, I know exactly what I'm doing.

48
00:03:31,256 --> 00:03:33,933
What's happened?
The time vortex, it's gone!

49
00:03:33,973 --> 00:03:36,151
That's impossible, it's just gone!

50
00:03:38,611 --> 00:03:40,909
Brace yourself! We're gonna crash!

51
00:03:46,601 --> 00:03:49,598
Everyone all right? Rose? Mickey?

52
00:03:49,638 --> 00:03:51,281
I'm fine. I'm OK, sorry, yeah.

53
00:03:55,468 --> 00:03:56,992
She's dead.

54
00:03:59,627 --> 00:04:02,230
The Tardis is dead.

55
00:04:02,063 --> 00:04:03,900
You can fix it.

56
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:06,258
There's nothing to fix.

57
00:04:06,298 --> 00:04:07,782
She's perished.

58
00:04:10,654 --> 00:04:16,490
The last Tardis in the universe,
extinct.

59
00:04:16,089 --> 00:04:18,806
We can get help, yeah?

60
00:04:18,846 --> 00:04:20,803
Where from?

61
00:04:20,843 --> 00:04:24,244
Well, we've landed, we've got to be
somewhere. We fell out of the Vortex.

62
00:04:24,284 --> 00:04:26,870
Through the Void, into nothingness.

63
00:04:27,596 --> 00:04:31,750
We're in some sort of no-place.

64
00:04:31,115 --> 00:04:33,829
The Silent Realm.

65
00:04:33,869 --> 00:04:36,741
The Lost Dimension.
Otherwise known as London.

66
00:04:40,382 --> 00:04:42,699
London, England, Earth.

67
00:04:42,739 --> 00:04:44,223
Hold on.

68
00:04:47,654 --> 00:04:49,254
The 1st of February, this year.

69
00:04:49,294 --> 00:04:50,850
Not exactly far flung, is it?

70
00:04:50,890 --> 00:04:52,655
So this is London? Yup.

71
00:04:52,695 --> 00:04:56,470
Your city? That's the one.
Just as we left it? Bang on.

72
00:04:56,087 --> 00:04:58,140
And that includes the zeppelins?

73
00:05:01,402 --> 00:05:03,199
What the hell? !

74
00:05:03,239 --> 00:05:05,598
That's beautiful.

75
00:05:05,638 --> 00:05:09,596
OK, so it's London with a big
International Zeppelin Festival.

76
00:05:09,636 --> 00:05:11,590
This is not your world.

77
00:05:11,630 --> 00:05:14,784
But if the date's the same. . .

78
00:05:14,824 --> 00:05:16,584
It's parallel, right?

79
00:05:16,624 --> 00:05:18,664
Am I right? Like a parallel
Earth where they've got zeppelins?

80
00:05:18,704 --> 00:05:20,779
Am I right? I'm right, aren't I?
Must be.

81
00:05:20,819 --> 00:05:24,378
So, a parallel world where. . . Oh,
come on! You've seen it on films.

82
00:05:24,418 --> 00:05:27,732
It's like an alternative to our
world, where everything's the
same, but a little bit different.

83
00:05:27,772 --> 00:05:32,897
Like, I dunno, traffic lights are
blue, Tony Blair never got elected.
And he's still alive.

84
00:05:35,167 --> 00:05:37,909
A parallel world and
my dad's still alive.

85
00:05:39,960 --> 00:05:43,517
Don't look at it, Rose. Don't even
think about it. It's not your world.

86
00:05:43,557 --> 00:05:46,475
But he's my dad. And. . .

87
00:05:46,515 --> 00:05:49,469
'Trust me on this. '

88
00:05:49,509 --> 00:05:51,386
Oh, that's weird.

89
00:05:51,426 --> 00:05:53,986
But he's real. . . 'Trust me on this. '

90
00:05:54,026 --> 00:05:58,859
He's a success. He was always
planning these daft little schemes,
health food drinks and stuff.

91
00:05:58,899 --> 00:06:00,661
Everyone said they were useless.

92
00:06:00,701 --> 00:06:04,335
But he did it. Rose. If you ever
trusted me, then listen to me now.

93
00:06:04,375 --> 00:06:06,291
Stop looking at him.

94
00:06:06,331 --> 00:06:10,647
Your father's dead, he died
when you were six months old. That's
not your Pete, that is a Pete.

95
00:06:10,687 --> 00:06:14,485
And for all we know he's got
his own Jackie, his own Rose.

96
00:06:14,525 --> 00:06:17,242
His own daughter, who's
someone else, but not you.

97
00:06:17,282 --> 00:06:20,757
You can't see him. Not ever.

98
00:06:20,797 --> 00:06:23,157
'Trust me on this.
Trust me on this. '

99
00:06:23,197 --> 00:06:26,690
'Trust me on this.
Trust me on this. '

100
00:06:52,367 --> 00:06:55,681
Hello? Sweetheart?

101
00:06:55,721 --> 00:06:57,838
Only me.

102
00:06:57,878 --> 00:07:00,632
Oh, the bad penny.
Was this your idea?

103
00:07:00,672 --> 00:07:03,839
Don't deny it, it's got your
fingerprints all over it.

104
00:07:03,879 --> 00:07:06,660
"Trust me on this!"
Oh, I can trust you, all right.

105
00:07:06,106 --> 00:07:10,207
Trust you to cock it up!
What have I done now? Have a look!

106
00:07:18,377 --> 00:07:20,774
What's wrong with that? 40!

107
00:07:20,814 --> 00:07:23,131
It says 40! You ARE 40.

108
00:07:23,171 --> 00:07:25,608
I don't want the whole
world telling, do I?

109
00:07:25,648 --> 00:07:28,245
You're having a party tonight.
My 39th!

110
00:07:28,285 --> 00:07:31,563
My official biography
says I was born on the same day
as Cuba Gooding Junior,

111
00:07:31,603 --> 00:07:35,438
and that makes
me 39, thank you very much. Rose!

112
00:07:35,478 --> 00:07:38,681
Come here! Rose!

113
00:07:38,721 --> 00:07:41,751
These are from the
girls in the office.

114
00:07:41,791 --> 00:07:44,387
Happy birthday.

115
00:07:44,427 --> 00:07:49,582
I've got hand-sculpted arrangements
by Veronica of Reykjavik, and your
secretary stopped off at a garage?

116
00:07:49,622 --> 00:07:53,179
I don't think so.
And if you're giving out
presents, where's my zeppelin?

117
00:07:53,219 --> 00:07:55,816
Everyone else has got one. Rose!

118
00:07:55,856 --> 00:07:57,456
Come on, Rose!

119
00:07:57,496 --> 00:07:59,613
Look, you didn't even notice,
did you? Special delivery.

120
00:07:59,653 --> 00:08:02,490
Got sent round today.

121
00:08:02,089 --> 00:08:08,444
Birthday present from Mr Lumic.
Latest model, diamond studded. They
pick up signals from Venezuela.

122
00:08:08,484 --> 00:08:10,801
Why would you want to pick
up signals from Venezuela?

123
00:08:10,841 --> 00:08:13,558
Well, I don't know!
But now I can find out!

124
00:08:13,598 --> 00:08:16,242
Oh, for God's sake, where is she?
Rose!

125
00:08:16,282 --> 00:08:18,750
She needs a good bath before
tonight or she's gonna be honking.

126
00:08:18,790 --> 00:08:21,115
Rose! Come to Mummy! Come on!

127
00:08:21,155 --> 00:08:23,746
There you are, my darling!

128
00:08:23,786 --> 00:08:26,223
PHONE RINGS

129
00:08:26,263 --> 00:08:31,580
Mr Lumic! Jackie was just saying
thank you, that's very kind of you.

130
00:08:31,098 --> 00:08:36,455
Those ear pods are hand made. Tell
her to take care. Course I will.

131
00:08:36,495 --> 00:08:41,368
Um, I don't suppose you'll be
joining us tonight for the party?
We'd be very honoured.

132
00:08:41,408 --> 00:08:44,892
The world below can party,
some of us have work to do.

133
00:08:44,932 --> 00:08:49,640
My plans have advanced, Peter. The
President has promised a decision.

134
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:54,111
I'm flying in now. We'll be at the
airstrip by five o'clock. Right.

135
00:08:54,151 --> 00:08:58,670
It's just that I promised
I'd help the wife out tonight.

136
00:08:58,107 --> 00:09:01,825
If the President of Great Britain
can make this meeting,
then so can you.

137
00:09:01,865 --> 00:09:03,662
I dunno, he's not
married to Jackie, is he?

138
00:09:03,702 --> 00:09:05,939
Five o'clock, Mr Tyler.

139
00:09:05,979 --> 00:09:07,497
A famous day!

140
00:09:09,704 --> 00:09:14,774
Voiceprint access, John Lumic.

141
00:09:14,814 --> 00:09:17,481
Initiate ear pod override.

142
00:09:18,967 --> 00:09:21,720
Subject, Jacqueline Tyler.

143
00:09:24,722 --> 00:09:26,246
Activate.

144
00:09:27,834 --> 00:09:29,474
Download.

145
00:09:29,514 --> 00:09:33,437
Tyler residence, February 1st.

146
00:09:33,477 --> 00:09:36,430
Give me security
arrangements for tonight.

147
00:09:36,470 --> 00:09:39,547
Passwords, encryption services.

148
00:09:48,535 --> 00:09:51,322
And restore.

149
00:09:56,728 --> 00:09:58,901
Thank you, Mrs Tyler.

150
00:10:00,486 --> 00:10:02,295
Activate picturelink.

151
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:06,834
Mr Crane? Mr Lumic.

152
00:10:06,874 --> 00:10:10,398
Events are moving
faster than I anticipated.

153
00:10:10,438 --> 00:10:14,389
I need extra staff. Very good, sir.

154
00:10:14,429 --> 00:10:16,357
I shall go on a recruitment drive.

155
00:10:17,863 --> 00:10:19,512
Back her up.

156
00:10:28,973 --> 00:10:31,291
I told you to keep an eye on her!

157
00:10:31,331 --> 00:10:33,495
She's all right.
She goes wandering off!

158
00:10:33,535 --> 00:10:36,208
A parallel world, it's
like a gingerbread house!

159
00:10:36,248 --> 00:10:38,860
All those temptations, calling her.

160
00:10:38,126 --> 00:10:40,601
Oh, so it's just Rose, then? There's
nothing out there to tempt me?

161
00:10:40,641 --> 00:10:45,599
Well, I don't know, I
can't worry about everything! If I
could just get this thing to. . .

162
00:10:45,639 --> 00:10:48,330
Did that help? Yes. Did that hurt?

163
00:10:48,073 --> 00:10:49,557
Yes. Ow.

164
00:11:27,432 --> 00:11:31,785
And it's good news for Great Britain,
as John Lumic returns to the
country of his birth.

165
00:11:31,825 --> 00:11:36,820
Mr Lumic,
the inventor of High Content Metal,
has denied allegations of ill-health.

166
00:11:36,860 --> 00:11:38,500
We're all flesh and blood.

167
00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:42,940
But the brain
is what makes us human.

168
00:11:42,134 --> 00:11:45,494
And my mind
is more creative than ever!

169
00:11:45,534 --> 00:11:48,975
'With shares in Cybus Industries
doubling in price, Mr Lumic. . . '

170
00:11:52,211 --> 00:11:58,635
'In other news. . . The Torchwood
Institute has published
a study, claiming that male. . . '

171
00:11:58,675 --> 00:12:01,192
We're not meant to be here.

172
00:12:01,232 --> 00:12:04,911
The Tardis draws its power
out of the universe, but
it's the wrong universe.

173
00:12:04,951 --> 00:12:08,467
It's like diesel in a petrol engine.

174
00:12:08,507 --> 00:12:13,822
But I've seen it in comics, people
go hopping from one alternative
world to another. It's easy!

175
00:12:13,862 --> 00:12:15,426
Not in the real world.

176
00:12:16,939 --> 00:12:19,375
Used to be easy.
When the Time Lords kept their eye

177
00:12:19,415 --> 00:12:23,893
on everything, you could pop between
realities, home in time for tea.

178
00:12:23,933 --> 00:12:26,350
Then they died and
took at all with them.

179
00:12:27,807 --> 00:12:30,924
The walls of reality closed.
The worlds were sealed.

180
00:12:30,964 --> 00:12:35,478
Everything became that bit less
kind. Then how did we get here?

181
00:12:35,518 --> 00:12:39,637
I don't know. Accident?

182
00:12:39,677 --> 00:12:42,219
Should've been impossible,
now we're trapped.

183
00:12:46,225 --> 00:12:48,143
What's that?

184
00:12:48,183 --> 00:12:49,744
What?

185
00:12:49,784 --> 00:12:51,962
That, there. Is that a reflection?

186
00:12:54,937 --> 00:12:58,574
It's a light!
Is it? Is that a light?

187
00:12:58,614 --> 00:13:02,934
I think that's a light,
that's all we need! We've got power!

188
00:13:02,974 --> 00:13:05,232
Mickey, we've got power! Ha!

189
00:13:12,997 --> 00:13:14,517
Gentlemen, gentlemen.

190
00:13:14,557 --> 00:13:20,352
I beg your attention. Christmas
has come early, so come and get it.

191
00:13:20,392 --> 00:13:23,267
Oh, smell that, comrades.

192
00:13:23,307 --> 00:13:28,586
Burgers and chips, sausage and beans,
pork, chicken and hot sweet tea.

193
00:13:28,626 --> 00:13:30,419
All for free!

194
00:13:30,459 --> 00:13:32,859
Don't go! But there's food.

195
00:13:32,899 --> 00:13:35,560
All you can eat, free of charge!

196
00:13:35,096 --> 00:13:38,615
Don't just stand there,
fill your faces with pasties, pies. . .

197
00:13:38,655 --> 00:13:40,575
They're lying. Easy for you to say.

198
00:13:40,615 --> 00:13:42,647
When did you last starve?

199
00:13:42,687 --> 00:13:45,488
All those people,
disappearing off the streets.

200
00:13:45,528 --> 00:13:47,604
It's been going on for months.

201
00:13:47,644 --> 00:13:49,238
It's them.

202
00:13:49,278 --> 00:13:52,519
They're taking you away. What for?

203
00:13:52,559 --> 00:13:54,193
Experiments.

204
00:13:54,233 --> 00:13:56,353
But hurry now, charity can't sleep. . .

205
00:13:56,393 --> 00:13:58,509
I'm so hungry.

206
00:13:58,549 --> 00:14:00,386
So, come and get it while it's hot!

207
00:14:00,426 --> 00:14:04,822
That's it, kind sir!
Get in there and fill your face!

208
00:14:04,862 --> 00:14:06,940
Feast yourself brother,

209
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:08,941
feast yourself.

210
00:14:08,981 --> 00:14:12,672
SHRIEKS AND CRIES
Take 'em away.

211
00:14:15,175 --> 00:14:17,973
It's alive!

212
00:14:18,013 --> 00:14:20,849
What is it? Nothing, it's tiny.
It's one of those

213
00:14:20,889 --> 00:14:23,486
insignificant little power cells
that no-one ever bothers about.

214
00:14:23,526 --> 00:14:25,319
And it's clinging on to life!

215
00:14:25,359 --> 00:14:28,160
It's got one little ounce of
reality, tucked away inside.

216
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:32,340
Enough to get us home? Not yet.

217
00:14:32,074 --> 00:14:33,638
I need to charge it up.

218
00:14:35,151 --> 00:14:37,188
We could go outside and lash
it up to the National Grid.

219
00:14:37,228 --> 00:14:39,909
Wrong sort of energy.
It's got to come from our universe.

220
00:14:39,949 --> 00:14:42,525
But we don't have anything.
There's me.

221
00:14:50,937 --> 00:14:53,814
I just gave away
ten years of my life.

222
00:14:53,854 --> 00:14:56,146
Worth every second.

223
00:15:05,319 --> 00:15:07,275
It's going out, is that OK?

224
00:15:07,315 --> 00:15:09,275
It's on a recharging cycle.

225
00:15:09,315 --> 00:15:12,949
It'll loop round, power back up
and be ready to take us home in. . .

226
00:15:12,989 --> 00:15:15,349
Ooh, 24 hours.

227
00:15:15,389 --> 00:15:17,667
So that gives us 24
hours on a parallel world.

228
00:15:17,707 --> 00:15:21,640
Shore leave. So long as we keep
our heads down, easy. No problem.

229
00:15:21,104 --> 00:15:22,780
Let's go and tell her.

230
00:15:22,820 --> 00:15:27,657
There you are! It's all right,
no applause, I fixed it.

231
00:15:27,697 --> 00:15:29,716
24 hours and then we're
flying back to reality.

232
00:15:33,171 --> 00:15:35,249
What is it?

233
00:15:35,289 --> 00:15:37,125
My phone connected.

234
00:15:37,165 --> 00:15:40,481
There's this Cybus network,
it finds your phone.

235
00:15:42,278 --> 00:15:44,917
And it gave me internet access.

236
00:15:44,957 --> 00:15:49,271
Rose, whatever it says,
this is the wrong world.

237
00:15:49,311 --> 00:15:53,290
I don't exist. What do you mean?
There's no Rose Tyler.

238
00:15:53,069 --> 00:15:55,105
I was never born.

239
00:15:55,145 --> 00:15:57,104
There's Pete, my dad, and Jackie.

240
00:15:57,144 --> 00:16:01,279
He still married Mum. But they never
had kids. Give me that phone.

241
00:16:02,740 --> 00:16:04,340
They're rich.

242
00:16:04,380 --> 00:16:08,390
They've got a house and
cars and everything they want.

243
00:16:10,211 --> 00:16:12,748
But they haven't got me.

244
00:16:15,887 --> 00:16:19,119
I've got to see 'em.

245
00:16:19,159 --> 00:16:24,236
You can't. I just want to see 'em.
I can't let you.
You just said 24 hours!

246
00:16:24,276 --> 00:16:27,674
You can't
become their daughter, that's not
the way it works. Mickey, tell her.

247
00:16:27,714 --> 00:16:31,308
24 hours, yeah? Where are you going?
Well, I can do what I want.

248
00:16:31,348 --> 00:16:34,240
I've got the address and everything,
Stay where you are, both of you!

249
00:16:34,064 --> 00:16:38,502
Come back! Mickey, come back here,
right now! I just want to see them.

250
00:16:38,542 --> 00:16:40,180
I've got things to see an' all.

251
00:16:40,058 --> 00:16:43,560
Like what? Well, you don't
know anything about me, do you?

252
00:16:43,096 --> 00:16:45,214
It's all about Rose.
I'm just the spare part.

253
00:16:45,254 --> 00:16:48,470
I'm sorry, I've got to go.

254
00:16:50,249 --> 00:16:52,886
Go on then.
There's no choice, is there?

255
00:16:52,926 --> 00:16:55,958
You can only chase after one of us.
It's never gonna be me, is it?

256
00:16:59,835 --> 00:17:03,674
Back here, 24 hours. Yeah.

257
00:17:03,714 --> 00:17:05,767
If I haven't found something better.

258
00:17:26,610 --> 00:17:31,641
Mr Tyler, what's the matter?
Couldn't wait until tonight?
Mr President, honoured.

259
00:17:31,681 --> 00:17:33,521
I'm on the fast-track programme.

260
00:17:33,561 --> 00:17:36,635
Cybus Industries have
bought up my company,
so I'm part of the firm now.

261
00:17:36,675 --> 00:17:39,354
Some people say they've
bought my government.

262
00:17:39,394 --> 00:17:40,954
I've never heard anybody say that.

263
00:17:40,994 --> 00:17:43,230
Never.

264
00:17:43,270 --> 00:17:47,908
You can trust me on this.
I tried your drink, that Vitex stuff.

265
00:17:47,948 --> 00:17:50,665
It tastes like pop. Well, it is pop.

266
00:17:50,705 --> 00:17:54,619
You made money by selling a health
food drink to a sick world.

267
00:17:54,659 --> 00:17:58,271
Not quite the ordinary Joe
you appear to be, are you?

268
00:18:00,333 --> 00:18:04,369
He does like to keep us waiting.

269
00:18:04,409 --> 00:18:10,240
But tell me, you've had a chance to
observe John Lumic more than most.

270
00:18:10,280 --> 00:18:12,598
What's your opinion?

271
00:18:12,638 --> 00:18:15,874
He's very sharp, I'd say.
Sharp as ever.

272
00:18:15,914 --> 00:18:18,872
Very clever man. Brilliant, in fact.

273
00:18:18,912 --> 00:18:20,909
Then you don't think he's insane?

274
00:18:20,949 --> 00:18:25,994
It's not the word I
would've used, no. I see.

275
00:18:34,938 --> 00:18:37,759
Can I see your ID, please?

276
00:18:39,054 --> 00:18:42,740
Yeah, that's in order.

277
00:18:47,603 --> 00:18:50,720
Am I all right to get past?
Yeah, no bother.

278
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:52,520
Curfew doesn't start till ten.

279
00:18:52,560 --> 00:18:55,194
There's a curfew? Course there is.

280
00:18:55,234 --> 00:18:58,550
Where've you been living, mate?
Up there with the toffs?

281
00:19:01,908 --> 00:19:03,956
I wish.

282
00:19:04,705 --> 00:19:07,370
See ya.

283
00:19:16,293 --> 00:19:18,306
Mickey's mum just couldn't cope.

284
00:19:20,250 --> 00:19:23,444
His dad hung around for a while, but
then he just sort of wandered off.

285
00:19:23,484 --> 00:19:26,241
He was brought up by his gran.

286
00:19:26,281 --> 00:19:29,598
She was such a great woman.

287
00:19:29,638 --> 00:19:32,793
God, she used to slap him !

288
00:19:32,833 --> 00:19:34,513
But then she died.

289
00:19:34,553 --> 00:19:36,870
She tripped and fell down the stairs.

290
00:19:36,910 --> 00:19:40,867
It was about five years ago
now, I was still in school.

291
00:19:40,907 --> 00:19:43,944
I never knew. Well, you never asked.
You never said.

292
00:19:43,984 --> 00:19:46,220
That's Mickey.

293
00:19:46,260 --> 00:19:47,818
I suppose I. . .

294
00:19:47,858 --> 00:19:50,446
We just take him for granted.

295
00:19:51,935 --> 00:19:54,892
Do you think she's alive, his gran?
Could be.

296
00:19:54,932 --> 00:19:57,809
Like I said, parallel world,
gingerbread house.

297
00:19:57,849 --> 00:20:00,300
We need to get out
here as fast as we can.

298
00:20:00,043 --> 00:20:02,710
TWO-TONE BEEPS

299
00:20:06,757 --> 00:20:08,955
What are they all doing?

300
00:20:08,995 --> 00:20:10,604
They've stopped.

301
00:20:27,897 --> 00:20:29,865
It's the earpieces.

302
00:20:31,414 --> 00:20:35,571
Like Bluetooth attachments, but
everyone's connected together.

303
00:20:35,611 --> 00:20:38,124
PHONE BEEPS

304
00:20:38,164 --> 00:20:42,119
It's on my phone. It's
automatic, look. It's downloading.

305
00:20:42,159 --> 00:20:44,576
Is that what they're all getting?

306
00:20:46,475 --> 00:20:51,233
News, international news,
sport, weather.

307
00:20:51,273 --> 00:20:53,470
They get it direct.

308
00:20:53,510 --> 00:20:56,106
Downloaded right into their heads.

309
00:20:56,146 --> 00:20:58,584
TV schedules, lottery numbers.

310
00:20:58,624 --> 00:21:01,166
Everyone shares the
same information.

311
00:21:03,059 --> 00:21:05,926
"The Daily Download,
published by Cybus Industries. "

312
00:21:14,643 --> 00:21:17,763
You lot, you're obsessed. You'll
do anything for the latest upgrade.

313
00:21:17,803 --> 00:21:20,641
Oi, they're not my lot.
Different world, remember?

314
00:21:20,681 --> 00:21:23,718
It's not so far off your world,
this place is only parallel.

315
00:21:23,758 --> 00:21:25,398
Ooh, look at that.

316
00:21:25,438 --> 00:21:30,351
Cybus Industries,
owners of just about every company
in Britain, including Vitex.

317
00:21:30,391 --> 00:21:34,367
Mr Pete Tyler's very well connected.

318
00:21:39,663 --> 00:21:42,689
OK, I give up. Let's go and see him.

319
00:21:59,279 --> 00:22:02,555
Who's that there?

320
00:22:04,313 --> 00:22:06,871
Who is it? I know you're there.

321
00:22:06,911 --> 00:22:10,828
Shame on you, tricking an old lady.
I've got nothing worth stealing.

322
00:22:10,868 --> 00:22:14,504
And don't think I'm gonna disappear,
you're not gonna take me!

323
00:22:14,544 --> 00:22:15,983
Hi.

324
00:22:17,739 --> 00:22:20,338
Is that you?

325
00:22:20,378 --> 00:22:22,335
It's me. I came home.

326
00:22:22,375 --> 00:22:24,530
Ricky? It's Mickey.

327
00:22:24,093 --> 00:22:27,372
I know my own grandson's name,

328
00:22:27,412 --> 00:22:30,773
it's Ricky. Now come here.

329
00:22:34,240 --> 00:22:36,398
OK. I'm Ricky.

330
00:22:36,438 --> 00:22:38,878
Course I am. Ricky, that's me.

331
00:22:38,918 --> 00:22:41,556
You stupid boy! Ow!
Where've you been?

332
00:22:41,596 --> 00:22:43,311
Stop hitting me!

333
00:22:43,351 --> 00:22:48,665
It's been days and days!
I keep hearing all these stories,
people disappearing off the street.

334
00:22:48,705 --> 00:22:54,619
There's nothing official on
the download. But there's all
these rumours and whispers.

335
00:22:54,659 --> 00:22:57,137
I thought they'd gone
and disappeared you!

336
00:22:57,177 --> 00:23:01,134
That carpet on the stairs, I told
you to get it fixed. You're gonna. . .

337
00:23:01,174 --> 00:23:04,331
fall and break your neck.
Well, you get it fixed for me!

338
00:23:04,371 --> 00:23:06,860
I should've done, way back.

339
00:23:06,126 --> 00:23:09,842
Guess I'm just kind of useless.
Now, I never said that.

340
00:23:09,882 --> 00:23:11,880
I am though.

341
00:23:11,920 --> 00:23:14,790
And I'm sorry, Gran. I'm so sorry.

342
00:23:14,119 --> 00:23:17,714
Don't talk like that. Do you know
what you need?

343
00:23:17,754 --> 00:23:21,514
A nice sit down and a cup of tea.

344
00:23:21,554 --> 00:23:25,147
You got time? For you, I've got
all the time in the world.

345
00:23:25,187 --> 00:23:28,705
Oh, you say that,
but you're all talk.

346
00:23:28,745 --> 00:23:32,819
It's those new friends of yours,
I don't trust them.

347
00:23:32,859 --> 00:23:35,699
What friends are they?
Don't pretend you don't know.

348
00:23:35,739 --> 00:23:38,416
You've been seen, Mrs Chan told me.

349
00:23:38,456 --> 00:23:42,567
Driving about, all helter-skelter
in that van. What van's that then?

350
00:23:42,607 --> 00:23:44,407
You know full well.

351
00:23:44,447 --> 00:23:47,808
Don't play games with me, now
get inside!

352
00:23:51,441 --> 00:23:54,387
I've been looking for you
everywhere! Ricky?

353
00:23:57,871 --> 00:24:00,590
Ricky?

354
00:24:00,630 --> 00:24:02,549
Ricky, you were the one who told us.

355
00:24:02,589 --> 00:24:05,147
You don't contact your family,
cos it puts them in danger.

356
00:24:05,187 --> 00:24:06,703
Yeah. Ricky said that.

357
00:24:06,743 --> 00:24:08,223
Course I did, just testing.

358
00:24:08,263 --> 00:24:10,420
I saw them, I taped them.

359
00:24:10,460 --> 00:24:13,978
They went round Blackfriars,
gathering up the homeless
like the Child Catcher.

360
00:24:14,018 --> 00:24:20,490
They must have took four dozen.
The vans were hired out to a company
called International Electromatics.

361
00:24:20,089 --> 00:24:22,325
But I did a protocol search.

362
00:24:22,365 --> 00:24:25,683
Turns out that's a dummy
company established by, guess who?

363
00:24:25,723 --> 00:24:27,523
I don't know, who?

364
00:24:27,563 --> 00:24:29,520
Cybus Industries!

365
00:24:29,560 --> 00:24:31,317
Well, now we've got evidence!

366
00:24:31,357 --> 00:24:33,914
Bad news is, they've
arrested Thin Jimmy.

367
00:24:33,954 --> 00:24:35,554
So that just leaves you.

368
00:24:35,594 --> 00:24:38,551
Leaves me what? The number one.

369
00:24:38,591 --> 00:24:42,226
Top of the list,
London's most wanted.

370
00:24:42,266 --> 00:24:44,945
OK, cool.

371
00:24:44,985 --> 00:24:46,942
Say that again.

372
00:24:46,982 --> 00:24:51,297
The most precious thing on
this earth is the human brain
and yet we allow it to die.

373
00:24:51,337 --> 00:24:55,249
But now, Cybus Industries has
perfected a way of sustaining

374
00:24:55,289 --> 00:25:00,800
the brain indefinitely, within a
cradle of copyrighted chemicals.

375
00:25:00,048 --> 00:25:05,959
The latest advances
in synapse research allows
cyberkinetic impulses

376
00:25:05,999 --> 00:25:08,359
to be bonded
onto a metal exoskeleton.

377
00:25:08,399 --> 00:25:10,755
This is the ultimate upgrade.

378
00:25:10,795 --> 00:25:14,592
Our greatest step into cyberspace.
I'm sorry,

379
00:25:14,632 --> 00:25:16,629
could we stop at that?

380
00:25:16,669 --> 00:25:22,304
I don't need the pitch.
I think we all know what this
ultimate upgrade entails.

381
00:25:22,344 --> 00:25:25,981
And I'm here to tell you, John,
the answer is no.

382
00:25:26,021 --> 00:25:30,375
My government does not
give you permission.

383
00:25:30,415 --> 00:25:32,972
And I think no government ever will.

384
00:25:33,012 --> 00:25:35,929
I've prepared a
paper for the Ethical Committee.

385
00:25:35,969 --> 00:25:40,442
Oh, come on! It's not just unethical,
it's obscene.

386
00:25:40,482 --> 00:25:44,800
Mr President,
if I might make a personal plea.

387
00:25:44,120 --> 00:25:45,876
I am dying, sir.

388
00:25:45,916 --> 00:25:47,795
I'm aware of that.

389
00:25:47,835 --> 00:25:49,515
And I'm very sorry.

390
00:25:49,555 --> 00:25:52,871
Without this project,
you have condemned me.

391
00:25:52,911 --> 00:25:57,386
My inventions have advanced
this whole planet.

392
00:25:57,426 --> 00:25:59,745
Would you have all that perish?

393
00:25:59,785 --> 00:26:02,183
You're a fine businessman, John.

394
00:26:02,223 --> 00:26:03,901
But you're not God.

395
00:26:05,816 --> 00:26:09,263
I'm really very sorry, but
I think we should end it there.

396
00:26:11,371 --> 00:26:13,967
Mr Tyler, I'll see you tonight.

397
00:26:14,007 --> 00:26:15,727
I think we could all do with a drink.

398
00:26:15,767 --> 00:26:18,594
Mr Lumic.

399
00:26:26,276 --> 00:26:31,430
Still, it's not the
only country in the world.
There's always New Germany.

400
00:26:31,470 --> 00:26:33,270
This is the homeland.

401
00:26:33,067 --> 00:26:35,467
My birthplace.

402
00:26:35,507 --> 00:26:38,950
You may leave. Thank you, sir.

403
00:26:43,498 --> 00:26:45,855
Mr Crane!

404
00:26:45,895 --> 00:26:49,409
Ready and waiting, sir.
Did the override work?

405
00:26:49,449 --> 00:26:51,447
Well, needs a bit of fine tuning.

406
00:26:51,487 --> 00:26:54,406
We weren't exactly delicate,
given the rush. But, yeah, yeah.

407
00:26:54,446 --> 00:26:56,963
It's all working.

408
00:26:57,003 --> 00:26:59,306
I've been testing the system, sir.

409
00:27:02,118 --> 00:27:05,554
They've
grafted on a treat, these have.

410
00:27:05,594 --> 00:27:07,871
Given a little persuasion.

411
00:27:07,911 --> 00:27:09,884
If you'd care to observe, sir.

412
00:27:12,266 --> 00:27:15,990
A-a-and, turn to the right.

413
00:27:17,303 --> 00:27:19,540
A-a-a-and, back to the left.

414
00:27:19,580 --> 00:27:22,457
Are you having fun, Mr Crane?

415
00:27:22,497 --> 00:27:24,451
No, no. Not at all, sir, no.

416
00:27:24,491 --> 00:27:27,212
It's just. . .well, it's irresistible.

417
00:27:27,252 --> 00:27:30,604
Then resist. And start the Upgrade.

418
00:27:30,644 --> 00:27:33,922
Did we get permission? I am governed
by greater laws, old friend.

419
00:27:33,962 --> 00:27:37,440
The right of a man to survive.

420
00:27:37,480 --> 00:27:38,998
Now begin.

421
00:27:40,916 --> 00:27:44,791
And turn to the left.

422
00:27:44,831 --> 00:27:46,520
Forward march.

423
00:27:50,107 --> 00:27:52,689
SHRIEKS

424
00:27:53,861 --> 00:27:55,621
Let's cover up that noise!

425
00:27:55,661 --> 00:27:57,421
Give us track number 19.

426
00:27:57,461 --> 00:28:01,812
MUSIC PLAYS:
# In the jungle
The mighty jungle

427
00:28:01,852 --> 00:28:05,900
# The lion sleeps tonight

428
00:28:05,049 --> 00:28:09,124
# In the jungle, the quiet jungle

429
00:28:09,164 --> 00:28:12,921
# The lion sleeps tonight

430
00:28:12,961 --> 00:28:14,958
# A wim-o-weh

431
00:28:14,998 --> 00:28:15,998
# A wim-o-weh

432
00:28:16,038 --> 00:28:17,635
# A wim-o-weh

433
00:28:17,675 --> 00:28:20,502
# A wim-o-weh

434
00:28:21,871 --> 00:28:22,911
# Ooh

435
00:28:22,951 --> 00:28:28,747
# A wee-um-um-a-weh. . . #

436
00:28:28,787 --> 00:28:31,819
MAN SCREAMS

437
00:28:35,260 --> 00:28:38,173
There's a light on.
There's someone inside the base.

438
00:28:38,213 --> 00:28:40,300
Mrs Moore, we've got visitors.

439
00:28:44,649 --> 00:28:47,925
One, two, three.

440
00:28:47,965 --> 00:28:49,764
Go!

441
00:28:49,804 --> 00:28:53,519
What the hell are you doing?

442
00:28:53,559 --> 00:28:55,116
What are you doing there?

443
00:28:55,156 --> 00:28:57,903
What am I doing here?

444
00:28:59,752 --> 00:29:01,606
What am I doing there?

445
00:29:09,065 --> 00:29:10,942
They've got visitors.

446
00:29:10,982 --> 00:29:14,893
February 1st. Mum's birthday.

447
00:29:14,933 --> 00:29:18,293
Even in a parallel universe,
she still loves a party.

448
00:29:18,333 --> 00:29:21,450
Well, given Pete Tyler's guest
list, I wouldn't mind a look.

449
00:29:21,490 --> 00:29:23,967
And there is one guaranteed
way of getting inside.

450
00:29:24,007 --> 00:29:26,913
Psychic paper! Who do you want to be?

451
00:29:39,430 --> 00:29:41,709
We could've been anyone.
It got us in, didn't it?

452
00:29:41,749 --> 00:29:45,623
You're in charge of the psychic
paper, we could've been guests.

453
00:29:45,663 --> 00:29:48,220
Celebrities, Sir Doctor, Dame Rose.

454
00:29:48,260 --> 00:29:49,740
We end up serving.

455
00:29:49,780 --> 00:29:52,940
I did enough of this back home.

456
00:29:52,134 --> 00:29:55,245
If you want to know what's
going on, work in the kitchens.

457
00:29:58,968 --> 00:30:03,484
According to Lucy,
that man over there. . . Who's Lucy?

458
00:30:03,524 --> 00:30:06,523
She's carrying the salmon pinwheels.

459
00:30:06,563 --> 00:30:08,519
Oh, that's Lucy, is it?

460
00:30:08,559 --> 00:30:12,354
Yeah. Lucy says, that
is the President of Great Britain.

461
00:30:12,394 --> 00:30:15,830
What, there's a President,
not a Prime Minister? Seems so.

462
00:30:15,870 --> 00:30:18,788
Or maybe Lucy's just a bit thick.

463
00:30:24,625 --> 00:30:27,142
Excuse me, thank you very much.

464
00:30:27,182 --> 00:30:29,656
Thank you. If I could have
your attention, please.

465
00:30:29,696 --> 00:30:32,773
Go on, Pete,
about time you did some work!

466
00:30:32,813 --> 00:30:34,627
I thought you liked 'em young?

467
00:30:36,127 --> 00:30:42,562
I'd like to say thank you to you all
for coming, on this
very special occasion.

468
00:30:42,602 --> 00:30:45,201
My wife's 39th.

469
00:30:45,241 --> 00:30:47,878
Don't believe that one!

470
00:30:47,918 --> 00:30:50,156
Trust me on this. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

471
00:30:50,196 --> 00:30:52,112
So, without any
further ado, here she is.

472
00:30:52,152 --> 00:30:57,550
The birthday girl,
my lovely wife, Jackie Tyler.

473
00:30:57,590 --> 00:31:00,337
THEY APPLAUD AND CHEER

474
00:31:05,580 --> 00:31:09,571
Now, I'm not giving a speech. That's
what my parties are famous for.

475
00:31:09,611 --> 00:31:14,986
No work, no politics,
just a few good mates
and plenty of black-market whisky.

476
00:31:16,490 --> 00:31:19,523
Pardon me, Mr President! So, yeah.

477
00:31:19,563 --> 00:31:21,600
Get on with it, enjoy, enjoy!

478
00:31:21,640 --> 00:31:25,411
APPLAUSE
CAMERA SHUTTERS WHIRR

479
00:31:25,477 --> 00:31:30,340
You can't stay, even if there
was some way of telling them.

480
00:31:30,074 --> 00:31:31,715
Of course I can't.

481
00:31:31,755 --> 00:31:34,188
I've still got Mum
at home, my real mum.

482
00:31:34,228 --> 00:31:36,281
I couldn't just leave her, could I?
It's just. . .

483
00:31:38,225 --> 00:31:41,262
They've got each other.

484
00:31:41,302 --> 00:31:45,107
Mum's got no-one.
She's got you, those two haven't.

485
00:31:46,534 --> 00:31:49,331
All these different worlds,
not one of them gets it right.

486
00:31:49,371 --> 00:31:52,732
Rose! Here's my little girl!

487
00:31:52,772 --> 00:31:56,440
Come to Mummy, come to Mummy!
Good girl!

488
00:31:56,084 --> 00:31:58,467
Good girl, aren't you?

489
00:32:02,599 --> 00:32:05,136
Sorry.

490
00:32:08,590 --> 00:32:15,302
'Platoon zero-two now boarding.
Platoon zero-two now boarding. '

491
00:32:18,304 --> 00:32:20,858
Stop staring. But what are they, sir?

492
00:32:20,898 --> 00:32:22,498
I'm told they're the future.

493
00:32:22,538 --> 00:32:25,253
Ain't technology wonderful?

494
00:32:25,293 --> 00:32:28,500
Go on, get to the cab, start her up.

495
00:32:28,090 --> 00:32:31,966
Sir.
MOBILE RINGS

496
00:32:32,006 --> 00:32:34,683
Mr Crane. . .are we mobile?

497
00:32:34,723 --> 00:32:37,680
Just about to hit the road, sir.
And yourself?

498
00:32:37,720 --> 00:32:39,637
I am arriving, Mr Crane.

499
00:32:39,677 --> 00:32:42,555
Prepare the factory.

500
00:32:42,595 --> 00:32:45,475
Now send them forth, old friend.

501
00:32:45,515 --> 00:32:47,330
Let the good work begin.

502
00:33:02,373 --> 00:33:04,330
He's clean. No bugs.

503
00:33:04,370 --> 00:33:09,408
This is off the scale. He's flesh
and blood. How did that happen?

504
00:33:09,448 --> 00:33:12,801
Well, it could be that Cybus
Industries have perfected

505
00:33:12,841 --> 00:33:17,272
the science of human cloning,
or your father had a bike.

506
00:33:21,513 --> 00:33:24,433
And your name's Mickey, not Ricky?

507
00:33:24,473 --> 00:33:25,911
Mickey.

508
00:33:25,951 --> 00:33:29,508
My dad was Jackson Smith,
he used to work at the key
cutter's on Clifton's Parade.

509
00:33:29,548 --> 00:33:31,112
He went to Spain, never came back.

510
00:33:33,138 --> 00:33:34,947
But that's my dad.

511
00:33:36,458 --> 00:33:39,535
So. . .we're brothers?

512
00:33:39,575 --> 00:33:41,574
Be fair, what else could it be?

513
00:33:41,614 --> 00:33:43,337
I don't know. . .

514
00:33:44,847 --> 00:33:49,123
But he doesn't just look like
me, he is exactly the same.

515
00:33:49,163 --> 00:33:54,321
Something else is going on here,
Jake. So who are you lot? We. . .

516
00:33:54,361 --> 00:33:56,118
We are the Preachers.

517
00:33:56,158 --> 00:33:58,340
As in Gospel Truth.

518
00:33:58,074 --> 00:33:59,632
You see?

519
00:33:59,672 --> 00:34:05,468
No Ear Pods. While rest of the world
downloads from Cybus Industries,

520
00:34:05,508 --> 00:34:11,361
we have got freedom. You're talking
to London's most wanted.

521
00:34:11,940 --> 00:34:16,613
But target number one is Lumic,
and we are going to bring him down.

522
00:34:16,653 --> 00:34:18,911
From your kitchen?

523
00:34:21,808 --> 00:34:23,846
Have you got a problem with that?

524
00:34:23,886 --> 00:34:25,859
No, it's a good kitchen.

525
00:34:27,882 --> 00:34:29,720
It's an upload from Gemini.

526
00:34:29,760 --> 00:34:32,120
Who's Gemini? The vans are back.

527
00:34:32,160 --> 00:34:33,877
They're moving out of Battersea.

528
00:34:33,917 --> 00:34:36,394
It looks like Gemini was right,

529
00:34:36,434 --> 00:34:38,234
Lumic's finally making a move.

530
00:34:38,274 --> 00:34:43,399
Then we are right behind him.
Pack up, we're leaving.

531
00:35:42,403 --> 00:35:44,451
SHE LAUGHS

532
00:35:48,997 --> 00:35:53,554
I remember her 21st,
a pint of cider in the George.

533
00:35:53,594 --> 00:35:56,630
Sorry. . .champagne?

534
00:35:56,670 --> 00:35:59,212
Might as well, I'm paying for it.

535
00:36:00,746 --> 00:36:02,979
A big night for you.

536
00:36:03,019 --> 00:36:04,980
For her.

537
00:36:05,020 --> 00:36:07,216
Still, she's happy.

538
00:36:07,256 --> 00:36:09,933
She should be. Great party.

539
00:36:09,973 --> 00:36:12,170
Do you think?

540
00:36:12,210 --> 00:36:14,900
You can trust me!

541
00:36:14,130 --> 00:36:17,567
"You can trust me on this!"
That's it, sorry, yeah.

542
00:36:17,607 --> 00:36:21,802
So, erm. . . how long have
you two been married?

543
00:36:21,842 --> 00:36:23,721
20 years.

544
00:36:23,761 --> 00:36:25,718
No kids or. . . ?

545
00:36:25,758 --> 00:36:28,160
We kept putting it off.

546
00:36:29,435 --> 00:36:31,892
She said she didn't
want to spoil her figure.

547
00:36:33,910 --> 00:36:37,187
It's not too late, she's only 40. 39.

548
00:36:37,227 --> 00:36:38,905
All right, 39.

549
00:36:41,342 --> 00:36:42,938
It's still too late.

550
00:36:42,978 --> 00:36:46,135
I moved out last month.

551
00:36:46,175 --> 00:36:48,968
But we're gonna keep it quiet,
you know, it's bad for business.

552
00:36:52,529 --> 00:36:56,487
Why am I telling you all this?
We haven't met before, have we?

553
00:36:56,527 --> 00:36:59,149
I don't know, you just
seem sort of. . . What?

554
00:37:01,080 --> 00:37:03,480
I don't know, just sort of. . .

555
00:37:04,958 --> 00:37:06,846
right.

556
00:37:08,872 --> 00:37:12,319
Stevie, how's things?
How's it going at Torchwood?

557
00:37:17,342 --> 00:37:20,817
I don't what they're doing,
but this seems to be the target.

558
00:37:20,857 --> 00:37:22,457
Big house, fair bit of money.

559
00:37:22,497 --> 00:37:24,894
Now we've got to find
a way to get in there.

560
00:37:24,934 --> 00:37:26,931
I've identified the address.

561
00:37:26,971 --> 00:37:32,287
It belongs to Peter Tyler,
the Vitex millionaire. Pete Tyler?

562
00:37:32,327 --> 00:37:36,428
He's listed as one of Lumic's
henchmen, a traitor to the state.

563
00:37:39,162 --> 00:37:42,290
But. . . We gotta get in there!
Shut it, duplicate,
that's what I just said.

564
00:37:44,276 --> 00:37:46,733
What are they doing?

565
00:37:50,070 --> 00:37:52,777
What the hell are they?
RHYTHMIC METALLIC BANGING

566
00:37:57,098 --> 00:38:03,162
LUMIC: 'The most precious thing
on this earth is the human brain. '

567
00:38:11,006 --> 00:38:15,410
Mrs Tyler, is there
anything I can get you?

568
00:38:15,081 --> 00:38:16,975
The last 20 years back.

569
00:38:19,837 --> 00:38:22,635
I can manage a glass of champagne,

570
00:38:22,675 --> 00:38:25,217
or a nice cup of tea?
Oh, that'd do me.

571
00:38:28,189 --> 00:38:31,664
My mum loves that.
End of a long night,
she never goes straight to bed.

572
00:38:31,704 --> 00:38:35,580
She always stays up just to have that
last cup of tea. Oh, I'm the same.

573
00:38:35,620 --> 00:38:37,577
Two sugars. And me.

574
00:38:37,617 --> 00:38:40,000
And Pete always says, "You know. . . "

575
00:38:41,614 --> 00:38:43,536
Ah, never mind him.

576
00:38:45,048 --> 00:38:46,889
I was talking to him earlier.

577
00:38:46,929 --> 00:38:49,284
He's a nice man.

578
00:38:49,324 --> 00:38:53,603
You know, a bit of a Jack the lad,
I suppose, even if he has got money,

579
00:38:53,643 --> 00:38:56,278
but he's a good bloke.

580
00:38:56,318 --> 00:38:58,815
Better than most.

581
00:39:00,713 --> 00:39:02,553
He's worth a second chance.

582
00:39:02,593 --> 00:39:05,908
Are you commenting on my marriage?

583
00:39:05,948 --> 00:39:09,186
No, I was just. . .
Who the hell do you think you are?

584
00:39:09,226 --> 00:39:13,818
You're staff. You're nothing
but staff, you're just the
serving girl, for God's sake.

585
00:39:13,858 --> 00:39:19,142
And you are certainly not getting
paid. Don't you dare talk to me.

586
00:39:39,795 --> 00:39:45,587
'This is the ultimate upgrade,
our greatest step into cyberspace. '

587
00:39:45,627 --> 00:39:46,656
Cybus!

588
00:40:12,960 --> 00:40:15,274
It's happening again.

589
00:40:15,314 --> 00:40:17,393
What do you mean?

590
00:40:17,433 --> 00:40:23,905
I've seen them before.
What are they? Cybermen!

591
00:40:23,945 --> 00:40:25,998
SCREAMING

592
00:40:42,685 --> 00:40:47,100
PHONE RINGS
Mr Lumic.

593
00:40:47,041 --> 00:40:53,155
Mr President. I suppose a
remark about crashing the party
would be appropriate at this point.

594
00:40:53,195 --> 00:40:54,716
I forbade this.

595
00:40:54,756 --> 00:40:56,316
These are my children, sir.

596
00:40:56,356 --> 00:40:58,627
Would you deny my family?

597
00:40:58,667 --> 00:41:00,904
What are they, robots?

598
00:41:00,944 --> 00:41:03,741
Worse than that.
Who were these people?

599
00:41:03,781 --> 00:41:06,418
It doesn't matter. They're people?

600
00:41:06,458 --> 00:41:10,894
They were - till they had
all their humanity taken away.

601
00:41:10,934 --> 00:41:15,849
It's a living brain jammed
inside a cybernetic body,
with a heart of steel,

602
00:41:15,889 --> 00:41:17,886
all emotions removed.

603
00:41:17,926 --> 00:41:19,646
Why no emotions?

604
00:41:19,686 --> 00:41:22,122
Because it hurts.

605
00:41:22,162 --> 00:41:25,360
I demand to know, Lumic!
These people, who were they?

606
00:41:25,400 --> 00:41:27,717
They were homeless
and wretched and useless,

607
00:41:27,757 --> 00:41:32,155
until I saved them and elevated them
and gave them life eternal.

608
00:41:32,195 --> 00:41:36,650
And now I leave you in their
capable hands. Good night, sir.

609
00:41:36,105 --> 00:41:38,738
Good night, Mr President.

610
00:41:40,222 --> 00:41:43,700
We have been upgraded. Into what?

611
00:41:43,740 --> 00:41:48,334
The next level of mankind.
We are Human 0.2.

612
00:41:48,374 --> 00:41:53,648
Every citizen will receive a free
upgrade, you will become like us.

613
00:41:53,688 --> 00:41:56,168
I'm sorry.

614
00:41:56,208 --> 00:41:59,565
I'm so sorry
for what's been done to you.

615
00:41:59,605 --> 00:42:01,801
But listen to me. . .

616
00:42:01,841 --> 00:42:06,117
This experiment ends! Tonight.

617
00:42:06,157 --> 00:42:08,836
Upgrading is compulsory.

618
00:42:08,876 --> 00:42:10,753
And if I refuse? Don't.

619
00:42:10,793 --> 00:42:12,666
What if I refuse?

620
00:42:12,706 --> 00:42:14,186
I'm telling you, don't!

621
00:42:14,226 --> 00:42:16,903
What happens if I refuse?

622
00:42:16,943 --> 00:42:20,541
Then you are not compatible.

623
00:42:20,581 --> 00:42:22,978
What happens then?

624
00:42:23,018 --> 00:42:25,720
You will be deleted.

625
00:42:30,292 --> 00:42:32,449
SCREAMS

626
00:42:32,489 --> 00:42:34,542
Jackie!

627
00:42:36,044 --> 00:42:37,773
Jackie!

628
00:42:47,355 --> 00:42:49,628
There's nothing we can do!

629
00:42:49,668 --> 00:42:52,950
My mum's in there!
She's not your mother, come on!

630
00:43:00,737 --> 00:43:03,160
Pete! Pete!

631
00:43:30,865 --> 00:43:32,585
Pete, is there a way out?

632
00:43:32,625 --> 00:43:34,261
The side gates!

633
00:43:34,301 --> 00:43:37,173
Who are you? How do you know so much?
You wouldn't believe it in a million years.

634
00:43:43,336 --> 00:43:45,409
Who's that?

635
00:43:45,449 --> 00:43:46,933
Get behind me!

636
00:43:58,361 --> 00:43:59,996
Oh, my God, look at you!

637
00:44:00,036 --> 00:44:03,316
I thought I'd never see you again!

638
00:44:03,356 --> 00:44:07,821
Yeah, no offence, sweetheart,
but who the hell are you? Rose!

639
00:44:09,383 --> 00:44:12,423
That's not me,
that's like. . .the other one.

640
00:44:12,463 --> 00:44:15,610
Oh, as if things weren't bad
enough, there's two Mickeys!

641
00:44:15,101 --> 00:44:17,598
It's Ricky.
But there's more of them.

642
00:44:20,851 --> 00:44:25,127
We're surrounded. Put the guns
down, bullets won't stop them.

643
00:44:25,167 --> 00:44:28,966
You! Stop shooting, now!

644
00:44:29,006 --> 00:44:31,322
We surrender!

645
00:44:31,362 --> 00:44:34,400
Hands up!

646
00:44:34,440 --> 00:44:36,797
There's no need to damage us.

647
00:44:36,837 --> 00:44:41,511
We're good stock. We volunteer
for the upgrade programme.

648
00:44:41,551 --> 00:44:45,745
Take us to be processed. You are
rogue elements. But we surrender.

649
00:44:45,785 --> 00:44:49,421
You are incompatible.
But this is a surrender!

650
00:44:49,461 --> 00:44:51,899
You will be deleted.

651
00:44:51,939 --> 00:44:53,896
But we're surrendering!
Listen to me, we surrender!

652
00:44:53,936 --> 00:44:55,616
You are inferior.

653
00:44:55,656 --> 00:45:01,270
Men will be reborn as Cybermen,
but you will perish
upon maximum deletion.

654
00:45:03,727 --> 00:45:07,254
Delete! Delete! Delete! Delete!
